From 9c53e731d86b7d5cd03bd9f96ba31aac8c0e8280 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nekokabu <nekokabu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 08:00:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] Fix parallel mode memory colour carry-over between scanlines

In 2-cycle mode the first blender cycle uses the memory colour of the
previous pixel the pipeline processed, so the first pixel of a span
blends with the value read last on the previous span (the pixel at
x = lx of the previous scanline, which wraps to the next row when the
span ends at the framebuffer's right edge). Without image read, fbread
keeps the colour channels, so they also carry over across primitives.

In parallel mode each worker renders every stride-th scanline and keeps
its own memory_color/pre_memory_color, so the carried value came from
scanline y - stride and from the worker's own history, and the pixel it
needed could belong to another worker's row, written or not depending on
timing. The first pixel of spans then differed from the single-threaded
renderer (and from real hardware), and varied from run to run.

Every worker now tracks both registers as the single-threaded renderer
has them between primitives and loads them before each 1/2-cycle
primitive. Framebuffer values that this needs are read by the worker
that owns the pixel's row (only it writes that row) at the start of the
primitive and handed over through a small mailbox in parallel.cpp.

Tested with a hardware test ROM (rdp_timing v3, 462 cases, dumps from
NTSC and PAL consoles): with 2, 3, 4, 5 and 8 workers, with and without
busy loop, the output is now byte-identical to Parallel = False and to
hardware in every 1-cycle and 2-cycle case; before, 9-12 cases differed.
